---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '@/data/site';

const links = [
  {
    label: 'GitHub',
    href: site.socials[0].href,
    external: true,
    note: 'The things I’ve loved building, and the little pieces of me left in each project.',
  },
  {
    label: 'LinkedIn',
    href: site.socials[1].href,
    external: true,
    note: 'The irony of having to prove you’re the best at what you do. Anyway, here we are.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Email',
    href: `mailto:${site.email}`,
    external: false,
    note: 'The email address I made as a kid. Still here, still mine. Kinda love that.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Resume',
    href: site.cv,
    external: true,
    note: 'My entire life, squeezed onto a single piece of paper. Good luck capturing it all.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Blog',
    href: '/journal/',
    external: false,
    note: 'I write some stuff sometimes. Thoughts, ideas, things I learn. Lol, read a few.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={site.title}
  description="Ayush Dev is a Computer Engineering student from Nepal who keeps exploring, building, playing, and learning."
>
  <article class="minimal-home">
    <h1>Ayush Dev</h1>
    <p>not the developer Dev, just Dev as my surname. Pretty interesting, right?</p>

    <p>Find me:</p>
    <ul>
      {links.map((link) => (
        <li>{link.note} "<a href={link.href} {...(link.external ? { target: '_blank', rel: 'noreferrer' } : {})}>{link.label}</a>"</li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <div class="about-copy">
      <p>Some things about me:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Computer Engineering student from Nepal</li>
        <li>Fascinated by the internet since I was a child
          <ul>
            <li>I didn’t just use it, I wanted to understand it</li>
            <li>Wandered its corners for years, reading things I barely understood</li>
            <li>Kept finding facts that made me go, “Wait… this is actually possible?”</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Starting my bachelor's made the curiosity stronger
          <ul>
            <li>It felt like finally entering a world I had only watched from the outside</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>I explore operating systems. I talked to CS people and asked what they used, and if someone mentions Linux, I install it. I try every new tool, framework, language, or database I hear about. I can’t leave interesting things alone: how does it work? Why do people use it? What can I build with it? Can I make it run on my own machine? That’s how I’ve spent years learning.</p>

      <p>But I’ve touched many things and never truly mastered one. I’ve built projects in different domains, tried different technologies and areas of computer science, and I know a little bit of almost everything. Other people have become really good at one thing, so what did I become? Jack of all trades, master of none.</p>

      <p>I love games. I’m not a professional, and I’ve never been particularly good at one game. I don’t play to be the best; in a strange way, it heals something in me. As a kid I could only watch others play and imagine what it felt like, because I didn’t have the kind of PC I have now. Now I finally have a good PC, so I play.</p>

      <blockquote><strong>Maybe I’m still searching for the answers<br />I couldn’t find at the back of the book.</strong></blockquote>
      <p class="projects-cta">check out my projects if u wanna see. <a href="/projects/">See</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
